Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.510 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  two summers ago I was sitting in my  living room blew it to the TV screen the  most important race of the Olympic Games  Rio 2016 was about to begin the men's  100-meter butterfly swim final now let  me set something straight I am NOT a  sports fan I barely even make it to  Arriaga gym and I live or just cross the  street but this race was different  the usual big names were swimming  Michael Phelps from the US shotlock Lowe  from South Africa they were the  favorites tout it to win it was going to  be a very tight race but these weren't  the guys I was watching the race for I  was rooting for a young boy called  Joseph schooling from a little country  called Singapore now I want you to watch  lane 4 with me just three in the pool  but we are history  that's Singapore to my little chameleon  place and then a little bit recon time  did you guys see that  my jump but also his win our first  Olympic gold as a country 50 years of  Independence  now first gold and I still remember the  pride I felt when they played our anthem  and raised our flag the tears that came  to my eyes and I was just sitting there  watching my TV screen there was probably  one of the proudest moments in our  country's young history but the odds  were stacked against us so few people  thought we will make it to the final  much less make it to the top but our boy  Joseph he had a dream David against  Goliath the little country that beat the  odds that day  Joseph fulfilled his own dream but he  also fulfilled the dreams of an entire  nation hoop in its hopes on him I'm not  a competitive athlete I'm a public  servant but in so many ways it's really  not that different I think about how  competitive my country is going to be in  the global race for talent resources  investments development I think about  what nation my country wants to be and  how we're going to get there how do you  build a nation a nation is built on  stories like Joseph's stories of dreams  your dreams mine our families our  communities but also the blood and the  sweat and the tears that we poured in to  get us to where we are even Martin  Luther King had a dream a nation is  built on all those dreams and the  discipline and the sacrifice they got us  here just 50 years ago Singapore was the  third world country nearly independent  from the British no natural resources  it was during this time my grandparents  raised a family when the country had  almost nothing there were no industries  no jobs it was about survival then not  about dreams my mom growing up remembers  one packet of noodles being shared  amongst five children one pair of shoes  passed down from child to child the by  the time we got to my youngest aunt  it was tattered and torn my grandfather  can only find employment as a clerk of  the police force and even then his  meager salary of $200 a month wasn't  enough to raise a family of six and so  he had to double up as a watchman at  night life was tough but that was life  then everyone thought that this new  country would fail you couldn't even  find us on the map read that small but  that didn't deter us our leaders were  determined that this little country we  would not just survive we would thrive  now the u.s. had 300 years of  development to get to where it was at  that time we didn't have that luxury of  time and so we embarked of  industrialization and globalization at  the same time because they knew then  that in order for this country to make  it they had to create employment create  economy but most of all create an  environment where people could hope  could aspire and could dream and so we  went out to look for companies you will  not look for investments and we realized  that the only way anyone was going to  invest in us was if you offered them  something that other people couldn't  stability transparency and a  pro-business environment it meant  everybody had to fall in line including  the unions  and so they came in droves some of our  earliest investors American companies  hewlett-packard Texas Instruments  they're still there today and they came  because of the political stability  because of the determination of the  people and because of the commitment of  the government so they built factories  hired thousands of people people  including my family members their  neighbors and their friends by their  very presence these companies required  local services and so that gave my  parents the chance to be small business  owners start their own company be their  own boss because that was their dream  and as Singapore came from third world  to first my family also moved up  socially and economically there by the  time I got to me I could be anything I  wanted to be that could be a doctor an  entrepreneur Miss Universe that was a  privilege I grew up with I speak three  languages I've lived on three continents  I love Shakespeare soliloquies as much  as I love Elvis as blue suede shoes and  I'm just as comfortable having burritos  for lunch at our bucco as I am having  Chinese food I am Who I am because of my  family and also because of my country a  country which today has a per capita GDP  comparable to that of the US and we're  just five and a half million people we  also have one of the most spectacular  skylines  not biased I didn't end up being a  doctor or an entrepreneur and I guess  not Miss Universe either I found my  calling in the public service where I  get to make people's dreams come true  whether they aspire to be that  entrepreneur that doctor start their own  business be the next Mark Zuckerberg  spend the rest of their lives coding in  front of a computer or networking with  the world's business leaders my job is  to make those dreams come true I get to  build industries I get to bring the  world's best companies to Singapore why  so that I can create the economy the  employment and the environment so that  these people's dreams can come true  so we've won the gold where do we go  from here  what got us here 50 years ago it's not  going to get us to next 50 years  economic empowerment brought us social  mobility but it also brought a whole  host of challenges like social  inequality and we have to address those  challenges as we build for the next 50  years the next hundred years as you  continue to build the country for people  to dream we started off as a nation that  beat the odds we did it with discipline  hard work sacrifice when Joseph's  schooling won that gold medal and he  brought it home he embodied not just our  dreams but all the values that make us a  nation 50 years ago we would never have  imagined the day we are one and on the  big gold medal and we did  with our dreams and with our values and  if Singapore can beat the odds this  little country then perhaps other cities  and other countries can too after all a  nation is built on dreams thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

